const salesRows=[['Sara','North','Notebooks',120],['Omar','South','Pens',340],['Lina','North','Pens',210],['Yusuf','East','Bags',95],['Mira','South','Notebooks',180],['Adam','East','Pens',260]];
L({id:'a11',t:'Excel: sort, filter & tables',goal:'Organise big lists: sort them, filter them, format them as tables and freeze the headings.',warm:'typing',warmLevel:5,
learn:[
 {h:'Sort',p:`<p>Click any cell in a column, then <b>Data → Sort</b> (A→Z or Z→A, smallest to largest or largest to smallest). Excel keeps each row together, so names stay with their numbers.</p>`,
  demo:DEMO(()=>xDesk(excelWin({A1:'Name',B1:'Region',C1:'Item',D1:'Sold',...Object.fromEntries(salesRows.flatMap((r,i)=>[['A'+(i+2),r[0]],['B'+(i+2),r[1]],['C'+(i+2),r[2]],['D'+(i+2),r[3]]]))},{cols:['A','B','C','D']})),[['to','c-D3'],['click'],['cls','c-D3','cs'],['to','x-sort'],['click'],['cap','Sort largest to smallest…',400],['cls','c-D3','cs',false,0],['fn',async p=>{const sorted=[...salesRows].sort((a,b)=>b[3]-a[3]);sorted.forEach((r,i)=>{['A','B','C','D'].forEach((c,j)=>{const e=p.q('c-'+c+(i+2));e.textContent=r[j];e.classList.add('flashc')})});await sleep(700);$$('.flashc',p.m).forEach(e=>e.classList.remove('flashc'))}],['cap','Top sellers first, and every row stayed together.']],{start:[50,60]})},
 {h:'Filter',p:`<p><b>Data → Filter</b> adds a little arrow to each heading. Click an arrow and untick values to <b>hide rows</b> you don't need right now, like showing only the North region. Nothing is deleted: clear the filter to see everything again.</p>`,
  demo:DEMO(()=>xDesk(excelWin({A1:'Name ▾',B1:'Region ▾',C1:'Item ▾',D1:'Sold ▾',...Object.fromEntries(salesRows.flatMap((r,i)=>[['A'+(i+2),r[0]],['B'+(i+2),r[1]],['C'+(i+2),r[2]],['D'+(i+2),r[3]]]))},{cols:['A','B','C','D']})+menu('flt',[['f1','☑ East'],['f2','☑ North'],['f3','☑ South']],30,30,true,'c-B1')),[['to','c-B1',5],['click'],['show','flt'],['cap','Untick East and South…',300],['to','f1'],['click'],['set','f1','☐ East'],['to','f3'],['click'],['set','f3','☐ South',400],['hide','flt'],['fn',async p=>{salesRows.forEach((r,i)=>{if(r[1]!=='North')['A','B','C','D'].forEach(c=>{p.q('c-'+c+(i+2)).style.opacity='.12'})})},600],['cap','Only North rows are shown. The rest are hidden, not deleted.']],{start:[50,60]})},
 {h:'Format as Table & freeze panes',p:`<ul><li><b>Home → Format as Table</b>: stripes, filter arrows and automatic formulas in one click. New rows join the table automatically.</li><li><b>View → Freeze Panes → Freeze Top Row</b>: the headings stay visible while you scroll down a long list.</li></ul>`}
],
mac:['Type (or paste) a list of at least 10 rows with 3–4 columns.','Sort it by one column, then by another.','Turn on Filter and show only one category.','Format it as a Table and freeze the top row.'],
practice:[{type:'sortfilter',title:'Sales detective',intro:'Click a heading to sort (click again to reverse). Use the drop-downs to filter.',head:['Name','Region','Item','Sold'],filter:[1,2],data:salesRows,tasks:[{text:'Sort by Sold, biggest first',check:s=>s.sortCol===3&&s.dir===-1},{text:'Show only the South region',check:s=>s.filt[1]==='South'},{text:'Show only Pens (any region)',check:s=>s.filt[2]==='Pens'&&!s.filt[1]},{text:'Sort names A→Z',check:s=>s.sortCol===0&&s.dir===1}]}],
challenge:'Make a list of 15 books, films or games with columns for type, year and rating. Format it as a table, sort by rating and filter by type.',
tell:'Show how filtering hides rows without deleting them.'});

const priceData={A1:'Item',B1:'Price',A2:'Notebook',B2:12,A3:'Pen',B3:3,A4:'Bag',B4:45,A5:'Ruler',B5:4};
L({id:'a12',t:'Excel: lookups & PivotTables',goal:'Look up values with XLOOKUP and summarise big tables with a PivotTable.',warm:'typing',warmLevel:5,
learn:[
 {h:'XLOOKUP: find a matching value',p:`<p>Got a price list and want Excel to fetch the right price? Use <b>XLOOKUP</b>:</p><p><b>=XLOOKUP(what to find, where to look, what to bring back)</b></p><p><b>=XLOOKUP("Bag",A2:A5,B2:B5)</b> → 45.</p><div class="tipbox"><b>Older Excel?</b> Use <b>=VLOOKUP("Bag",A2:B5,2,FALSE)</b>: find "Bag" in the first column of A2:B5 and bring back column 2.</div>`,
  demo:DEMO(()=>xDesk(excelWin(Object.assign({},priceData,{D1:'Find',E1:'Price',D2:'Bag'}))),[['to','c-E2'],['click'],['cls','c-E2','cs'],['type','c-E2','=XLOOKUP(D2,A2:A5,B2:B5)',45],['cls','c-D2','rng'],['cls','c-A4','rng'],['cls','c-B4','rng',true,700],['keys',['return'],600],clearRng,['set','c-E2','45'],['style','c-E2',{justifyContent:'flex-end',fontWeight:'700'}],['cap','Excel found "Bag" and brought back its price.',800],['to','c-D2'],['click'],['set','c-D2','Pen',300],['set','c-E2','3',600],['cap','Change the item and the price updates.']],{start:[50,60]})},
 {h:'PivotTables: summarise in seconds',p:`<p>A <b>PivotTable</b> turns hundreds of rows into a neat summary, like total sales per region.</p><p>1. Click inside your data. 2. <b>Insert → PivotTable</b> → OK. 3. Drag <b>Region</b> to Rows and <b>Sold</b> to Values. Done!</p>`,
  demo:DEMO(()=>xDesk(excelWin({A1:'Name',B1:'Region',C1:'Sold',...Object.fromEntries(salesRows.flatMap((r,i)=>[['A'+(i+2),r[0]],['B'+(i+2),r[1]],['C'+(i+2),r[3]]]))},{cols:['A','B','C','D','E'],extra:`<div class="pivot off" data-k="pv"><b>Sum of Sold</b><div><span>Row labels</span><span>Total</span></div><div data-k="pv1"><span>East</span><span>355</span></div><div data-k="pv2"><span>North</span><span>330</span></div><div data-k="pv3"><span>South</span><span>520</span></div><div class="tot"><span>Grand total</span><span>1205</span></div></div>`})),[['to','t-insert'],['click'],['set','rtools',`<span class="rbig" data-k="i-pivot">▦ PivotTable</span>${insertTools}`,300],['to','i-pivot'],['click'],['cap','Region → Rows, Sold → Values…',600],['show','pv',800],['cap','Total sales per region, in seconds!']],{start:[50,60]})},
 {h:'Excel habits of pros',p:`<ul><li>One table per sheet, headings in row 1, no empty rows in the middle.</li><li>Never type a number twice: point at the cell instead.</li><li>Name your sheets (double-click the tab at the bottom).</li><li>Check formulas with {{<kbd>⌃</kbd> + <kbd>&#96;</kbd>||<kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>&#96;</kbd>}} (show formulas).</li></ul>`}
],
mac:['Make a small price list and use XLOOKUP (or VLOOKUP) to fetch a price.','Change the item you search for and watch the result change.','Make a PivotTable that totals a list by category.','Rename your sheets with clear names.'],
practice:[{type:'sheet',title:'Shop helper',intro:'Use XLOOKUP and SUMIF. Text needs "quotes".',cols:['A','B','C','D'],rows:[['Item','Price','Find:','Bag'],['Notebook',12,'Price:',''],['Pen',3,'',''],['Bag',45,'Pens sold:',''],['Ruler',4,'',''],['Sales','','',''],['Pen',3,'',''],['Bag',45,'',''],['Pen',3,'','']],res:['D2','D4'],tasks:[{cell:'D2',expect:45,text:'D2: find the price of the item in D1 with =XLOOKUP(D1,A2:A5,B2:B5)',has:'LOOKUP'},{cell:'D4',expect:6,text:'D4: total money from Pens in A7:B9 with =SUMIF(A7:A9,"Pen",B7:B9)',has:'SUMIF'}],help:'<b>Tip:</b> after it works, change D1 to "Pen" and watch D2 update.'}],
challenge:'Make a shopping list where you type an item name and Excel looks up its price, then totals the list. Add a PivotTable of spending by category.',
tell:'Explain XLOOKUP using a real example, like looking up a phone number.'});
